<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
            http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      290 — account.localpart: the account-takeover-by-collision fix.

      BUG: two DIFFERENT (provider, handle) accounts can derive the SAME homeserver
      (Matrix/Mastodon) localpart via ServiceTokenBroker.matrixUser (this jar) /
      ServiceTokenBroker.localpart (user-bff-api's own copy) — both just
      handle.split("@")[0].toLowerCase() then strip non [a-z0-9._=/-]. Changeset
      181-casdoor-provider.xml relaxed ck_account_provider to allow 'google', 'discord'
      AND 'casdoor' to coexist, so e.g. google:alice@x.com and casdoor:alice@y.com are
      BOTH legitimate rows that fold to the same localpart 'alice'. Because both BFFs'
      ServiceTokenBroker register-or-logins against the homeserver with ONE SHARED
      service password, whichever account is PROVISIONED SECOND silently password-logs
      into the FIRST account's already-registered homeserver identity — a real
      account-takeover-by-collision (not hypothetical; the account table has always
      permitted the colliding rows, only nothing has ever checked for the collision).

      FIX (provision-time uniqueness, not provider-namespacing — see the fuller
      rationale in AuthController / InternalAccountController / AccountLocalpartService):
      persist the RESOLVED, collision-checked localpart on the account row itself, at
      the ONE place every account is created (all 3 creation call sites are in THIS
      repo/THIS DB — registerGoogle, registerDiscord, InternalAccountController#ensureAccount),
      and have every homeserver-mint call site (AuthController#provision here;
      MatrixProxyController/MastodonProxyController in user-bff-api, over the internal
      account-read API) READ that persisted value instead of re-deriving it from
      whatever identity string happens to be at hand (Account.handle(), a Casdoor JWT
      claim, ...) at mint time.

      DELIBERATELY NULLABLE (no NOT NULL here): AccountStore.ensureAccount (auth-core,
      a versioned EXTERNAL dependency — see the pom.xml dependency comment; not
      modified by this changeset, to avoid a version-bump chain through auth-core's
      other consumer) INSERTs a fresh account row via Spring Data R2DBC's AccountRow
      entity, whose generated INSERT column list does NOT include this new column (the
      entity has no localpart field). A NOT NULL here without a column DEFAULT would
      therefore fail EVERY account creation outright; a DEFAULT would collide with the
      UNIQUE constraint the second any two accounts were created. Postgres UNIQUE
      already permits any number of NULL rows (NULLs are pairwise distinct for
      uniqueness purposes), so nullable + UNIQUE is the correct combination: the
      window between "row inserted" and "localpart resolved+persisted" is a few
      milliseconds within the SAME request (AccountLocalpartService.resolveAndPersist
      runs immediately after store.ensureAccount(...) in registerGoogle/
      registerDiscord/InternalAccountController#ensureAccount, before the HTTP
      response is returned), and AuthController#provision() cannot be reached for an
      account until its SEPARATE registration-complete step runs afterward — so a real
      caller can never observe a null localpart on their own account.

      BACKFILL: derives each EXISTING row's localpart with the exact same sanitizer
      (regexp_replace/split_part mirror ServiceTokenBroker.matrixUser/localpart's
      handle.split("@")[0].toLowerCase() + strip-non-[a-z0-9._=/-] + empty-&gt;"user"),
      then deterministically disambiguates any collision AMONG EXISTING rows: the
      account table carries no created_at column (only updated_at, which IS rewritten
      on some later writes — e.g. AccountStore#ensureAccount(provider,handle,roles)
      rewrites it on every native-login sign-in — so it is an imperfect but the ONLY
      available proxy for "first created"; a true fix would need a new created_at
      column, a materially bigger schema change out of scope for this fix), tie-broken
      by id for full determinism, so the migration is reproducible. The first-created
      keeps the bare candidate; later collisions get an id-hash suffix rather than
      failing the migration. Verified against this checkout's own test/seed fixtures
      before writing this changeset: no cross-provider handle collisions exist in any
      committed fixture data (compose-stack personas are single-provider e2e-*
      handles) — this backfill logic is exercised by DataConstraintsTest /
      AccountStoreTest's real-Postgres slice tests regardless, so it is verified
      against live Postgres semantics (window functions, regexp_replace, split_part),
      not just reasoned about.

      CROSS-PARTITION SUFFIX-COLLISION HARDENING (round-2 review, low-severity, fixed
      while already in this file): the first draft suffixed later collisions with a
      small sequential integer (candidate || '-' || rn, i.e. 'alice', 'alice-2', ...).
      That is disambiguated WITHIN one candidate's own partition, but two DIFFERENT
      partitions can still coincide AFTER suffixing — e.g. a group of 'alice' rows
      contributes a literal 'alice-2', which can collide with a wholly separate
      pre-existing row whose OWN bare candidate is genuinely 'alice-2' (its own
      partition, rn=1) — and the UNIQUE constraint below would then fail the
      migration. Suffixing with a short hash of the row's own globally-unique id
      instead of a small dense integer makes that cross-partition coincidence
      astronomically less likely (still not a mathematical impossibility — the
      UNIQUE constraint remains the actual backstop and would fail the migration
      loudly, never silently, on the residual chance) without requiring a recursive/
      iterative disambiguation loop in pure SQL, which is not a "cheap" fix.

      Band 290: next free above 280. Appended LAST.
    -->

    <changeSet id="290-account-localpart-add-column" author="hugmesh">
        <comment>account.localpart — nullable (see rationale above), the resolved
                 collision-checked homeserver localpart for this account.</comment>
        <addColumn tableName="account">
            <column name="localpart" type="varchar(320)"/>
        </addColumn>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="290-account-localpart-backfill" author="hugmesh">
        <comment>Backfill localpart for pre-existing rows: same sanitizer as
                 ServiceTokenBroker.matrixUser/localpart, collisions among EXISTING
                 rows deterministically disambiguated by updated_at/id order, later
                 collisions suffixed with a short hash of the row's own globally-unique
                 id (not a small sequential integer — see the cross-partition
                 suffix-collision hardening note above) so this never fails the
                 migration on the common case.</comment>
        <sql>
            WITH candidates AS (
                SELECT id, updated_at,
                       CASE
                           WHEN regexp_replace(lower(split_part(handle, '@', 1)), '[^a-z0-9._=/-]', '', 'g') = ''
                           THEN 'user'
                           ELSE regexp_replace(lower(split_part(handle, '@', 1)), '[^a-z0-9._=/-]', '', 'g')
                       END AS candidate
                FROM account
            ), ranked AS (
                SELECT id, candidate,
                       ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY candidate ORDER BY updated_at ASC, id ASC) AS rn
                FROM candidates
            )
            UPDATE account a
            SET localpart = CASE WHEN r.rn = 1 THEN r.candidate
                                  ELSE r.candidate || '-' || substr(md5(r.id), 1, 8) END
            FROM ranked r
            WHERE a.id = r.id;
        </sql>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="290-account-localpart-unique" author="hugmesh">
        <comment>UNIQUE(localpart) — the actual enforcement boundary (NULLs exempt,
                 see rationale above); the collision check at each creation call site
                 is the application-level guard, this is the DB-level backstop.</comment>
        <addUniqueConstraint tableName="account" columnNames="localpart"
                             constraintName="uq_account_localpart"/>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
